/*
 * phosh on a Surface Duo: keep the shell out of the hinge.
 *
 * THIS IS A TEMPLATE. sfduo-shell-css fills in the tokens (a name between
 * at-signs) for the output scale in phoc.ini and writes
 * /usr/share/sfduo/gtk.css; the package does it
 * at build time for the scale it ships, and `sudo sfduo-shell-css` does it
 * again after a change of scale. The numbers below are for @W@x@H@ logical
 * (scale @SCALE@).
 *
 * Both panels are one output, @W@x@H@ logical. The 84 physical pixels of
 * hinge between them are addressable and physically hidden: logically they
 * are the column [@LEFT@, @RIGHT@]. gmobile's display-panel description tells
 * phosh where they are, but phosh consults it only when it places the top
 * bar's clock. Everything else the shell centres - the lock screen clock, the
 * home bar handle, the search field - centres on the middle of the bezel.
 *
 * So each centred widget is given a left margin of @RIGHT@px: the near panel
 * plus the hinge. What was centred on the whole screen is then centred on the
 * right panel, under the thumb of a right hand holding the device.
 *
 * Margins land only on widgets GTK3 allocates through a CSS gadget, and only
 * on names the widget actually carries: GtkBuilder ids are not visible to CSS
 * here (#box_info matches nothing), so every selector below is either an
 * element name from phosh's own stylesheet, a style class from its .ui files,
 * or a name set with gtk_widget_set_name.
 */

/* ---- top bar and the shade -------------------------------------------- */

/* Nothing here any more. The clock used to sit in the bezel, and a padding on
   the group left of it pushed the clock onto the right panel. The patched
   shell (phosh-patches/0004) gives a display with a seam a top bar per half
   instead: each is @LEFT@px wide, each centres its own clock inside its own
   panel, and either can be pulled down on its own. Nudging the clock now
   would push it off the half it belongs to.

   That patch also needs the hinge described to it, which is what
   display-panels/qcom,sm8150-mtp.json does; sfduo-phosh-install puts the
   description down with the patched binary and takes it away with it. On
   stock phosh, without the description, both bars become one again and its
   clock goes back to the middle of the bezel - a rescue path, not a mode
   anyone lives in. */

/* One status bar, not two. Each half has a bar of its own, and both carried
   the same clock, the same signal and the same battery: side by side that
   reads as one bar drawn twice, not as two halves. It showed on the lock
   screen first, where the bar is only indicators and the pair of them sat a
   hinge apart.

   So the far half's bar is emptied and kept. Emptied, not removed: the strip
   is what the finger pulls, and the shade behind it is the whole point of
   splitting the panel. It is transparent rather than hidden so the strip
   keeps its height and stays where the drag starts. Only the folded bar goes
   - the unfolded shade is a different page of the same stack and draws in
   full on both halves. */
.phosh-far-half #top-bar { opacity: 0; }

/* No bar at all on the lock screen. That screen has a clock of its own, big
   and on the right panel, and a row of indicators above it on the left panel
   only said the same thing twice - and said it on one half of a device whose
   halves are meant to match.

   It goes out with the screen and comes back with the unlock, over a quarter
   of a second: the transition belongs to the style being moved *to*, so the
   locked rule turns it off on the way in and the rule above brings it in
   gently on the way out. phosh-patches/0004 puts the class on the panel. */
#top-bar { transition: opacity 250ms ease-out; }
.phosh-locked #top-bar { opacity: 0; transition: none; }

/* ---- lock screen ------------------------------------------------------ */

/* The clock, the date and the notifications share one box, and GTK places a
   child with a margin inside it by a rule that is not "the box minus the
   margin" - as long as there is slack, that is: margin plus width less than
   the box. So there is no slack. The box is given the display's width, and
   each child is given a margin of one panel plus the hinge and a width of
   exactly the other panel: @RIGHT@ + @LEFT@ = @W@, nothing left to
   distribute, and the child sits where the arithmetic says. Clock and date
   on the right panel, under the thumb; the notifications on the left, where
   the eyes are and no hand is.

   The arrow, the hint under it and the keypad live in boxes of their own and
   never needed this: @RIGHT@ puts them on the right panel's centre as it is.

   All of it is the landscape width, like every other number here. */
phosh-lockscreen .p-large { min-width: @W@px; }

#phosh-lockscreen-clock,
#phosh-lockscreen-date {
  margin-left: @RIGHT@px;
  min-width: @LEFT@px;
}

phosh-lockscreen scrolledwindow {
  margin-left: 0;
  margin-right: @RIGHT@px;
}

/* The arrow above the hint, and the whole passcode page - status line, the
   entry, the keypad and the button - which is one centred box. */
.phosh-lockscreen-arrow,
.phosh-lockscreen-unlocker {
  margin-left: @RIGHT@px;
}

/* The hint under the arrow has no name of its own, only "dim-label" - and so
   does the artist line in the media player, which shares this screen, hidden
   in a revealer. A revealer that shows nothing still asks for its child's
   width, so a margin on that label made the player ask for more than the
   panel, the area holding the notifications could shrink no further, and the
   card sat right of centre with the clock dragged along. Selecting the hint
   by where it lives - a direct child of a direct child of the deck's page -
   reaches nothing else. */
phosh-lockscreen deck > box > box > label.dim-label { margin-left: @RIGHT@px; }

/* ---- home bar --------------------------------------------------------- */

/* The drag handle is 200px wide and centred, i.e. entirely inside the bezel:
   the one gesture target the device cannot show. The handle itself is a
   GtkEventBox, which has no CSS gadget and so ignores a margin outright; the
   box that centres it is an ordinary GtkBox and does not. Only the handle
   moves - the event box behind it still spans the whole width, so the swipe
   still starts anywhere along the bottom edge. */
#home-bar { margin-left: @RIGHT@px; }

/* ---- the desktop ------------------------------------------------------ */

/* Phosh has no home screen. With nothing running it shows the app grid, and
   there is no state in which the device shows a wallpaper and nothing else -
   which is what this device wants, since its launcher is the dock along the
   bottom. So the grid is pushed past the bottom edge: it is still there, still
   reachable by search when the margin is removed, but nothing of it is on the
   screen and nothing of it can be touched. @OFFGRID@ is twice the longer
   side, more than the screen in either orientation.

   The alternative was to cover the grid with the dock, which worked and cost
   the notification shade: pulled down, it unfolded underneath the cover and
   could not be reached. */
phosh-app-grid { margin-top: @OFFGRID@px; }

/* ---- app grid --------------------------------------------------------- */

.phosh-search-bar,
#phosh-filter-adaptive-btn {
  margin-left: @RIGHT@px;
}

/* The grid itself keeps both panels; only its columns move. A flow box fits
   as many equal columns as the child's minimum width allows, and an odd count
   always puts one column astride the hinge - at the icons' natural width that
   is seven columns, with the fourth in the bezel. Six columns instead, and
   the gap between the third and the fourth lands on the middle of the seam.

   The minimum that produces six is the child's whole width, padding included:
   @GRIDMIN@ + 2x18 = @GRIDCHILD@, against @GRIDROW@ of usable row and 6px of
   column spacing - six of them fit, seven do not. The padding is the other
   half of the trick - it insets each icon far enough that the two columns
   beside the gap stop drawing well before the bezel starts. */
phosh-app-grid-button,
phosh-app-grid-folder-button {
  min-width: @GRIDMIN@px;
  padding-left: 18px;
  padding-right: 18px;
}

/* The top bar is never see-through. phosh makes it transparent whenever no
   window is maximized - over a wallpaper that is the point, over this black
   one it changes nothing - but a new window maps at the very top of the
   output for a moment, under the bar, before it is tiled: its header bar
   showed through as a grey flash across the status icons on every launch
   (caught at 5 fps: one frame of "Settings" and a menu button behind the
   clock). The launch curtain cannot cover it - the bar is not the dock's. */
#top-bar-bin,
#top-bar-bin:not(.p-solid) {
  background-color: #000000;
  transition: none;
}
